<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  ⚠️ NOT WIRED UP YET — AND TURNING IT ON IS A HUMAN-ONLY STEP.
	  CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS is deliberately NOT set in project.pbxproj. Setting
	  it fails the build outright:
	      Provisioning profile "iOS Team Provisioning Profile: *" doesn't
	      include the Increased Memory Limit capability.
	  The capability has to be enabled on the App ID in the Apple Developer
	  portal (Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles → the com.templetongroup.radiant
	  identifier → Increased Memory Limit) and the profile regenerated. That
	  needs Tony's account; no agent can do it. Once it is enabled, add
	      CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS = App/App.entitlements;
	  to both build configurations and rebuild.

	  ⚠️ THIS ENTITLEMENT IS WHAT MAKES THE LARGER MODELS POSSIBLE AT ALL.

	  iOS gives an ordinary app only a fraction of the device's RAM before
	  jetsam kills it — on a 12 GB iPhone that can be under half. A 4 GB model
	  needs about 5 GB resident, so without this the top of the catalogue is
	  permanently "won't run" on hardware that is physically capable of it.

	  With it, iOS raises this process's limit toward the device maximum. It
	  changes what os_proc_available_memory() reports, which is the number the
	  fit labels are computed from — so removing it does not just lose
	  capability, it silently re-labels most of the catalogue.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
